      "latency_ms": 1731.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 732,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – water is most dense at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats. |\n| **Occurrence** | • Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of the planet’s water is saltwater in oceans).<br>• Freshwater (rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater) makes up < 3 % of total water. |\n| **Biological role** | • Constitutes ~60–70 % of the human body by weight.<br>• Acts as a medium for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients and waste, regulates temperature, and provides structural support in cells. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation.<br>• Agriculture (irrigation).<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines).<br>• Manufacturing (cooling, solvents, chemical synthesis). |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat.<br>• Influences weather patterns, ocean currents, and climate regulation. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n1. **Polarity** gives it strong hydrogen‑bonding, which underlies many of its unusual physical properties (high boiling point, surface tension, etc.).\n2. **Hydrogen bonding** creates a dynamic network that can reorganize quickly, allowing water to act as a “lubricant” for molecular motion in biological systems.\n3. **Thermal stability** helps maintain relatively stable temperatures in organisms and ecosystems.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) with remarkable physical and chemical properties that make it an unparalleled medium for life, weather, and countless human activities. Its ability to exist in all three states under Earth‑like conditions, its high heat capacity, and its role as a universal solvent are the key reasons it is often called the “universal life‑supporting fluid.”"
